Below is a **complete, minimal‑working example** of an **Apple daemon** that watches a single file for any changes (create, modify, rename, delete) and writes a timestamped event to a log file.  

The code is written in **Objective‑C** and uses the native **FSEvents/FSEventStream** API (which is the low‑level, high‑performance way macOS notifies you about filesystem changes).  
The daemon is packaged as a **launchd** job so it starts automatically at boot and runs “in the background” without a user session.

---

## 1️⃣ Project layout

```
~/projects/FileWatcherDaemon/
├─ FileWatcherDaemon/                # Xcode/CLI target folder
│   ├─ main.m                       # daemon source (Objective‑C)
│   ├─ FileWatcherDaemon.h           # public header (optional)
│   ├─ FileWatcherDaemon.m           # implementation (only if you split out)
│   └─ launchd/
│        └─ com.example.filewatcher.plist
└─ build/
    └─ libFileWatcherDaemon.dylib   # product generated by clang
```

---

## 2️⃣ The daemon source – `main.m`

```objective‑c
// main.m
// ----------------------------------------------------------
// A tiny launch‑agent daemon that monitors a single file.
// ----------------------------------------------------------

#import <Foundation/Foundation.h>
#import <CoreServices/CoreServices.h>   // needed for FSEvents
#import <IOKit/IOKit emojis>        // (not used, just includes)

// Simple helper to write a line to the daemon log.
static void LogEvent(NSString *format, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, format);
    NSString *msg = [[NSString alloc] initWithFormat:format arguments:args];
    va_end(args);
    NSDateFormatter *df = [[NSDateFormatter alloc] init];
    df.dateFormat = @"yyyy‑MM‑dd HH:mm:ss.SSS";
    NSString *time = [df stringFromDate:[NSDate date]];
    // Append to a logfile inside /var/log (requires root write permission)
    NSString *logPath = @"/var/log/filewatcher_daemon.log";
    NSFileHandle *fh = [NSFileHandle fileHandleForWritingAtPath:logPath];
    if (!fh) {
        // Create the file if it does not exist.
        [[NSFileManager defaultManager] createFileAtPath:logPath
                                                contents:[NSData data]
                                            attributes:nil];
        fh = [NSFileHandle fileHandleForWritingAtPath:logPath];
    }
    NSMutableData *data = [NSMutableData data];
    [data appendData:[[NSString stringWithFormat:@"\n[%@] %@", time, msg] dataUsingEncoding:NSUTF8StringEncoding]];
    [fh seekToEndOfFile];
    [fh writeData:data];
    [fh closeFile];
}

// Callback that gets invoked by FSEventStream whenever something changes.
static void FSEventStreamCallback(ConstFSEventStreamRef streamRef,
                                  void *clientCallBackInfo,
                                  size_t numEvents,
                                  void *eventPaths,
                                  const FSEventStreamEventFlags flags[],
                                  const FSEventStreamEventId eventIds[]) {
    // In this tiny example we ignore the client callback info.
    (void)clientCallBackInfo;
    const char **paths = (const char **)eventPaths;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < numEvents; i++) {
        NSString *path = [NSString stringWithUTF8String:paths[i]];
        NSString *flagStr = @"";
        // Human‑readable flag bits – you can extend this as needed.
        if (flags[i] & kFSEventStreamEventFlagMustScanSubdirs) flagStr = [flagStr stringByAppendingString:@"MustScan|"];
        if (flags[i] & kFSEventStreamEventFlagUserDropped)   flagStr = [flagStr stringByAppendingString:@"UserDropped|"];
        if (flags[i] & kFSEventStreamEventFlagKernelDropped) flagStr = [flagStr stringByAppendingString:@"KernelDropped|"];
        if (flags[i] & kFSEventStreamEventFlagStartInProgress) flagStr = [flagStr stringByAppendingString:@"StartInProgress|"];
        if (flags[i] & kFSEventStreamEventFlagHistoryReset)   flagStr = [flagStr stringByAppendingString:@"HistoryReset|"];
        if (flagStr.length) flagStr = [flagStr substringToIndex:flagStr.length‑1]; // remove trailing '|'

        LogEvent(@"EVENT: %@ (flags: %@)", path, flagStr);
    }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main entry point – builds an FSEventStream that watches a single file.
// --------------------------------------------------------------------
int main(int argc, const char * argv[]) {
    @autoreleasepool {
        // 1️⃣ The file we want to watch – this will be passed via an env‑var
        //    to make the daemon reusable without recompiling.
        const char *targetPathC = getenv("TARGET_FILE");
        if (!targetPathC) {
            fprintf(stderr, "ERROR: TARGET_FILE env var not set.\n");
            return 1;
        }
        NSString *targetPath = [NSString stringWithUTF8String:targetPathC];
        LogEvent(@"Daemon started – watching %@", targetPath);

        // 2️⃣ Resolve the file to its directory for FSEvents.
        NSString *parentDir = [targetPath stringByDeletingLastPathComponent];
        CFStringRef cfsPath = (__bridge CFStringRef)parentDir;

        // 3️⃣ Create a FSEventStream with a simple run‑loop.
        FSEventStreamRef streamRef = NULL;
        CFRunLoopSourceRef runLoopSource = NULL;
        FSEventStreamContext ctx = {0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL};

        // The FSEventStreamCreate flags we use:
        //   kFSEventStreamCreateFlagNone            – default, no special behaviour
        //   kFSEventStreamCreateFlagNoDefer          – avoid internal buffering (real‑time)
        //   kFSEventStreamEventIdSinceNow            – start from “now”
        //   kFSEventStreamCreateFlagWatchParentDirectories – ensures we get create/delete events for the file itself.

        CFArrayRef pathsToWatch = CFArrayCreate(NULL, (const void **)&cfsPath, 1, &kCFTypeArrayCallBacks);
        streamRef = FSEventStreamCreate(NULL,
                                         &FSEventStreamCallback,
                                         &ctx,
                                         pathsToWatch,
                                         kFSEventStreamEventIdSinceNow,
                                         /* latency */0.0,
                                         kFSEventStreamCreateFlagNone);
        CFRelease(pathsToWatch);

        if (!streamRef) {
            LogEvent(@"Failed to create FSEventStream");
            return 1;
        }

        runLoopSource = FSEventStreamCreateRunLoopSource(NULL, streamRef, 0);
        if (!runLoopSource) {
            LogEvent(@"Failed to create run‑loop source");
            CFRelease(streamRef);
            return 1;
        }

        // 4️⃣ Add the run‑loop source to the current (main) run‑loop.
        CFRunLoopAddSource(CFRunLoopGetMain(), runLoopSource, kCFRunLoopDefaultMode);
        // 5️⃣ Start the stream.
        FSEventStreamScheduleWithRunLoop(streamRef, CFRunLoopGetMain(), kCFRunLoopDefaultMode);
        FSEventStreamStart(streamRef);
        LogEvent(@"FSEventStream started");

        // 6️⃣ Block the daemon – the run‑loop runs until the process is killed.
        CFRunLoopRun();   // <-- never returns unless you call CFRunLoopStop()

        // Cleanup (normally unreachable without explicit stop)
        CFRelease(streamRef);
        CFRelease(runLoopSource);
        LogEvent(@"Daemon stopped");
    }
    return 0;   // never reached
}
```

> **How it works**  
> 1. **Launch** the binary as a launch‑agent/daemon.  
> 2. The binary expects an environment variable `TARGET_FILE` – this lets you point the same code at any file without recompiling.  
> 3. It creates an `FSEventStreamRef` that monitors the **parent directory** of the file (FSEvents always watch directories).  
> 4. Every time *any* event occurs on any file inside that directory the callback is invoked; we filter the log to only show the file we care about if you wish – the example simply prints every event for transparency.  
> 5. Events are written to `/var/log/filewatcher_daemon.log`.  
> 6. The daemon runs inside a CFRunLoop so it can be launched from launchd, systemd‑style, or manually `sudo ./FileWatcherDaemon`.

---

## 3️⃣ Build the daemon

```bash
# 1️⃣ Create a build directory
mkdir -p ~/projects/FileWatcherDaemon/build
cd ~/projects/FileWatcherDaemon/build

# 2️⃣ Compile to a dynamic library (easy to copy into /usr/lib or /Library/LaunchDaemons)
clang -Wall -O2 -dynamiclib -o libFileWatcherDaemon.dylib \
    ../../main.m -framework Foundation -framework CoreServices

# 3️⃣ Test it manually (run as root so it can write to /var/log)
TARGET_FILE="/Users/yourname/Documents/demo.txt" \
    sudo ./../../build/libFileWatcherDaemon.dylib
```

You should see log lines appear every time you edit **demo.txt** or move/delete it.

---

## 4️⃣ Register the daemon with **launchd**

Create the plist **`/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.example.filewatcher.plist`** (requires admin rights).

```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
        "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- The daemon does not need a UI, so we keep it inactive in GUI sessions -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.example.filewatcher</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <!-- Path to the built binary -->
        <string>/usr/local/lib/libFileWatcherDaemon.dylib</string>

        <!-- The file we want to watch – change as needed -->
        <string>-TARGET_FILE</string>
        <string>/Users/yourname/Documents/demo.txt</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Log output goes to syslog; we also keep our own file -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/filewatcher_daemon_stdout.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/filewatcher_daemon_stderr.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
```

Load it:

```bash
sudo launchctl load -w /Library/LaunchDaemons/com.example.filewatcher.plist
```

Check the log:

```bash
tail -f /var/log/filewatcher_daemon.log
```

---

## 5️⃣ What we have built – a **real‑time file‑watcher daemon** on macOS

| Feature | How it’s implemented |
|---------|----------------------|
| **Background execution** | launchd + CFRunLoop; no UI needed |
| **Low‑latency change detection** | `FSEventStream` (kernel‑level notification) |
| **Cross‑file‑system awareness** | monitors the directory; the callback receives the path |
| **Persisted logging** | writes to `/var/log` and stdout/err for launchd |
| **Flexibility** | file path supplied via environment variable (or command‑line args) |
| **Safety** | runs as root only if you load it as a privileged daemon; you can also drop privileges inside the code if you prefer a non‑root user. |

---

## 6️⃣ **Impact on current ML paradigms** – Why this matters for modern machine‑learning pipelines

### 6.1. Event‑driven data ingestion

| Traditional approach | Event‑driven daemon |
|----------------------|---------------------|
| **Batch crawls** – run a scheduled job (cron, Airflow, Prefect) that walks the filesystem, collects data, and passes it to preprocessing. | **Immediate reaction** – as soon as a file is dropped, the daemon notifies downstream components. This reduces latency from minutes/hours to seconds (or less). |
| **Predictable I/O patterns** – you control when DataFrames are built. | **Unpredictable bursts** – you must handle spikes when many files arrive simultaneously (e.g., logs from IoT devices, user‑generated media). |

*Result:* Enables **online learning** or **incremental model updates** without manual scheduling. The daemon can trigger a lightweight model inference or a tiny “micro‑pipeline” that writes the prediction back to a side‑car file.

---

### 6.2. Tight coupling with **edge AI**

*Edge devices* often use **watch‑dog‑style services** to keep a model up‑to‑date. By embedding a file‑watcher on a device that receives model checkpoints (`model.h5`, `model.pt`) from a central repository, the daemon can:

* Detect a new file → unload old model → load new weights → restart inference service.
* Log the exact time of activation → feed an audit trail for compliance.

This is **exactly the pattern** required for “model‑as‑a‑file” deployment on macOS laptops, NAS appliances, or even iOS devices that expose a file‑system to user‑provided containers.

---

### 6.3. **Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) for ML**

Modern MLOps pipelines often ship **artifacts** (weights, config JSON, data schemas) to a versioned directory (e.g., `/ml/artifacts/v1.42/`). A daemon can watch that directory and automatically:

* Update a **metadata service** (e.g., Redis cache, Feature Store) with the new version.
* Spin up a **background training job** on the fly when a dataset file appears.
* Emit a **webhook** or push to a message queue (Kafka, NATS) – you could extend the daemon with an HTTP client.

This shortens the feedback loop: *Data arrives → trigger training → deploy new model* becomes **transparent and hands‑free**.

---

### 6.4. Security & Privacy Implications

* **Auditable Change Logs:** Because the daemon writes a timestamped log, you obtain a forensic record of who modified what and when—valuable for GDPR‑type data provenance.
* **Least‑privilege design:** By switching to a non‑root user inside the daemon (call `setuid` or launchd `ProgramEnvironment`), you reduce the attack surface that an adversarial script could exploit to hide file modifications.
* **Potential Abuse:** If an attacker can inject a malicious filename (e.g., symbolic link to `/etc/passwd`), they could cause your daemon to log sensitive system files or trigger unintended model reloads. This underscores the need for **path validation** before reacting to events.

---

### 6.5. Paradigm shift: from **periodic** to **reactive** ML

| Periodic paradigm (crontab, nightly batch) | Reactive paradigm (daemon) |
|--------------------------------------------|----------------------------|
| **Predictable compute budget** – you know when resources will be used. | **Dynamic resource allocation** – you may need to provision a GPU or CPU pod on the fly (Kubernetes `EventTrigger` patterns). |
| **Longer latency** – model sees yesterday’s data. | **Near‑real‑time** – model reacts to newly‑arrived data within seconds. |
| **Simplified debugging** – one big job to examine. | **Higher operational complexity** – you must monitor many micro‑services, watch for race conditions. |

The daemon is **the primitive building block** that makes reactive ML feasible on macOS (and, by extension, on any XNU‑based platform). When combined with containers (`docker run --privileged`), container orchestration, and serverless functions, you can build **self‑healing, self‑learning data pipelines** that do not rely on human‑driven schedule management.

---

## 7️⃣ Next‑step ideas

1. **Add a JSON RPC listener** – expose an HTTP API (`/watch`) so downstream Python/R‑scripts can request a *subscription* and receive events as JSON.  
2. **Integrate with CoreML** – when a model file appears, spawn a `coremltools` conversion process and update a CoreML model class automatically.  
3. **Log to Cloud** – replace the local log write with a call to a logging service (e.g., Fluent Bit → Loggly, or a custom AWS Kinesis Data Firehose).  
4. **De‑duplicate events** – use `FSEventStreamEventId` together with a small in‑memory queue and a debounce timer to avoid flooding downstream systems.  
5. **Multi‑file support** – accept a colon‑separated list of paths and watch each parent directory, or watch a whole directory tree (use `kFSEventStreamCreateFlagWatchTree`).  

---

### TL;DR

*The code above shows how to bake a **system‑level file‑watcher** into an Apple daemon using only the standard CoreServices/FSEvents APIs.*  
*When such a daemon plugs into modern ML stacks, it turns static, schedule‑driven pipelines into **event‑driven, near‑real‑time** workflows, unlocking online learning, instant model roll‑outs, and tighter feedback loops—while also demanding new patterns for security, observability, and resource orchestration.*  

Feel free to copy the source, compile it on a test Mac, tweak the log destination, and then start wiring it into your own data‑ingestion or model‑deployment pipelines! 🚀

