Below is a **complete, defensive‑programming‑style solution** that you can drop into any macOS environment and run as a **launch‑daemon**.  
It consists of three parts:

| Part | Language | What it does |
|------|----------|---------------|
| **1️⃣ AppleScript wrapper** (optional) | AppleScript / AppleScript‑ObjC | Starts a small Objective‑C helper when the script is launched, so you can edit the file directly from *Script Editor*. |
| **2️⃣ Objective‑C helper** | Objective‑C (Cocoa) | Registers with **FSEvents** and watches a single file for changes. It logs every modification to *system.log* and can fire a callback that updates any downstream database you own. |
| **3️⃣ launchd plist** | XML | Declares the helper as a permanent background daemon that starts at boot and restarts automatically on failures. |

---

## 1️⃣ Why Objective‑C + FSEvents (instead of pure AppleScript)?

* **Granular, low‑latency monitoring** – FSEvents is the kernel‑level API Apple provides for file‑system change notification. AppleScript can only poll or rely on `osascript -e "tell application \"Finder\" …"` which is slow, fragile, and incurs unnecessary Finder traffic.  
* **Error‑handling & type safety** – Objective‑C’s exception handling, `NSError` propagation and compile‑time checks make it much easier to write defensive code.  
* **Integration with modern macOS frameworks** – You can log, dispatch to other services, or push notifications without leaving the language you already know.  

> **Bottom line:** Use the “AppleScript wrapper” only as a convenience for people who want to launch the daemon from the Script Editor. The real work is done by the compiled Objective‑C binary.

---

## 2️⃣ AppleScript *launch‑wrapper* (optional)

```applescript
(* file: LaunchMonitor.scpt *)
set targetApp to POSIX file "/usr/local/bin/FileWatcher" -- compiled binary from part 2
set launchAgentPath to "/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.mycompany.filewatcher.plist"

try
    do shell script ¬
        "/usr/bin/launchctl unload " & launchAgentPath user privileges
    do shell script ¬
        "/usr/bin/launchctl load " & launchAgentPath user privileges
    display notification ¬
        "FileWatcher daemon re‑loaded." with title "FileWatcher"
on error errMsg number errNum
    display alert "Launch error" message errMsg as critical
end try
```

*Save this as `LaunchMonitor.scpt`*.  
Running the script (`osascript LaunchMonitor.scpt`) will **unload & reload** the launch daemon – handy for a quick “restart‑on‑the‑fly” in development.

> **Defensive note:** The wrapper runs with `user privileges` to avoid sandboxing problems when the script is executed by a standard user. In production you’d likely want to run the daemon as `root` (see the plist below).

---

## 3️⃣ Objective‑C helper – “FileWatcher”

Create a new **Cocoa Command Line Tool** in Xcode (or just write the `.m` file and compile with clang). The source file is `FileWatcher.m`:

```objective‑c
//
//  FileWatcher.m
